  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given that the line $y=x+k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$",
  "worked_solution": "To determine whether the line $y=x+k$ intersects the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ y=x+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & =x+k \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+1-k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,\\,b=-1,$ and $c=1-k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-1)\\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}-4(1)(1-k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{1-4+4k}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{4k-3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4k-3 & <0 \\\\ 4k & <3 \\\\ k & <\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<\\frac{3}{4}.$",
